---
import KnowledgeCheck from "../../../components/KnowledgeCheck.astro";
import LoopbackCapture from "../../../components/LoopbackCapture.astro";
import CourseLayout from "../../../layouts/CourseLayout.astro";

const home = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
const frames = [
  {
    number: 1,
    source: 40001,
    destination: 5002,
    message: "PING",
    length: 12,
    meaning: "The client sends a four-byte message.",
  },
  {
    number: 2,
    source: 5002,
    destination: 40001,
    message: "PONG",
    length: 12,
    meaning: "The server program sends a four-byte reply.",
  },
  {
    number: 3,
    source: 40001,
    destination: 5002,
    message: "BYE",
    length: 11,
    meaning:
      "The client sends three more bytes. The exercise sends no reply to BYE.",
  },
];
---

<CourseLayout
  page="wireshark"
  title="Wireshark UDP lab | Computer Networks"
  description="Read a UDP capture, identify the two endpoints, and tell application replies apart from transport behavior."
>
  <div class="intro lab-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Wireshark lab · UDP</p>
    <h1>
      Read a <span>UDP capture</span>
    </h1>
    <p>
      Follow three datagrams on loopback. Find the reply the server program
      chose to send and the message it received without replying.
    </p>
    <div class="intro-links">
      <a
        class="button primary"
        href={home + "captures/udp-hello.pcap"}
        download
      >
        Download the UDP capture
      </a>
      <a class="button" href="#live-capture">
        Capture your own UDP traffic
      </a>
      <a class="button" href={home + "labs/wireshark/"}>
        Read the TCP lab
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="lab-section" aria-labelledby="udp-open">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="udp-open">Open and filter the sample</h2>
      <p>
        The teaching capture models the same messages as the downloadable
        practice script. Its timestamps are illustrative.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ol class="lab-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>File → Open.</strong> Open <code>udp-hello.pcap</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Show both directions.</strong> Enter{" "}
        <code>udp.port == 5002</code> in the display filter bar. You should see
        all three frames.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Show the messages sent to the server.</strong> Change the filter
        to <code>udp.dstport == 5002</code>. Frames 1 and 3 remain. Use{" "}
        <code>udp.srcport == 5002</code> to see the server reply.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Focus on one conversation.</strong> Restore the two-way filter,
        select a frame, then choose <strong>Follow → UDP Stream</strong>.
        Wireshark groups the exchange even though UDP establishes no transport
        connection. For this sample, use <code>udp.stream == 0</code> to show
        the conversation again. Use the packet list to see each datagram
        boundary.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="lab-section" aria-labelledby="udp-frames">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="udp-frames">Read the datagram fields</h2>
      <p>
        Expand <strong>User Datagram Protocol</strong> in Packet Details. The IP
        addresses are both <code>127.0.0.1</code>. The source and destination
        ports tell you which program sent each message.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="table-hint">Scroll the table sideways to see every column.</p>
    <div
      class="table-scroll"
      tabindex="0"
      role="region"
      aria-label="Captured UDP datagrams"
    >
      <table class="comparison-table reliability-table">
        <caption>
          UDP exchange between client port 40001 and server port 5002
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Frame</th>
            <th scope="col">Source port</th>
            <th scope="col">Destination port</th>
            <th scope="col">UDP Length</th>
            <th scope="col">Payload</th>
            <th scope="col">What happened</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {frames.map(
            ({ number, source, destination, length, message, meaning }) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{number}</th>
                <td>{source}</td>
                <td>{destination}</td>
                <td>{length}</td>
                <td>
                  <code>{message}</code>
                </td>
                <td>{meaning}</td>
              </tr>
            ),
          )}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <ul class="lab-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Length.</strong> UDP Length counts the eight-byte UDP header as
        well as the payload. A length of 12 means four bytes of application
        data.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Payload.</strong> Select the UDP payload or Data entry to
        highlight the bytes. Look for <code>PONG</code> in the ASCII portion of
        Packet Bytes for frame 2.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Replies.</strong> The practice server sends PONG in response to
        PING. UDP itself has no handshake, ACK, sequence number, or automatic
        retry. Use your program’s logs to tell intentional silence from a
        message that never arrived.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <KnowledgeCheck
      id="check-udp-capture-length"
      question="A UDP frame shows Length 11. How many bytes are application data?"
      choices={[
        {
          label: "3 bytes",
          correct: true,
          feedback:
            "Subtract the eight-byte UDP header. In frame 3 the data is BYE.",
        },
        {
          label: "11 bytes",
          feedback:
            "UDP Length includes the header. Check the payload separately.",
        },
      ]}
    />
    <KnowledgeCheck
      id="check-udp-capture-silence"
      question="What can you conclude when a captured UDP request has no visible reply?"
      choices={[
        {
          label: "UDP guarantees the request was delivered.",
          feedback:
            "UDP provides no delivery confirmation. Check the receiver or its logs.",
        },
        {
          label:
            "The capture shows no reply. More evidence is needed to explain why.",
          correct: true,
          feedback:
            "The program may have chosen silence, a datagram may have been lost, or your capture may have missed it. In this exercise the server log confirms receipt of BYE.",
        },
      ]}
    />
  </section>

  <LoopbackCapture protocol="udp" port={5002}>
    <p>
      Download{" "}
      <a href={home + "examples/udp/hello.py"} download>
        hello.py
      </a>
      . With the capture running, open a terminal in the download folder and run
      <code>python3 hello.py</code>. The terminal confirms that the server
      receives both PING and BYE, and that the client receives PONG.
    </p>
  </LoopbackCapture>

  <section class="lab-section" aria-labelledby="udp-check">
    <h2 id="udp-check">Troubleshoot a missing datagram or reply</h2>
    <ul class="lab-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>No UDP rows.</strong> Look for the script’s terminal output
        first. Clear the display filter and check that you started on the
        loopback interface before the script ran.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Only requests appear.</strong> If you used{" "}
        <code>udp.dstport == 5002</code>, switch back to{" "}
        <code>udp.port == 5002</code> to see replies.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The sender says it sent a message.</strong> Compare with the
        receiver’s log. In this example, the receiver prints BYE and
        intentionally sends no reply. A capture from the sending side alone
        cannot confirm receipt.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="lab-section" aria-labelledby="udp-next">
    <h2 id="udp-next">Compare it with TCP</h2>
    <p>
      Open the TCP sample alongside this one. First compare how each exchange
      starts. Then compare a TCP ACK with the PONG text carried inside a UDP
      datagram.
    </p>
    <div class="intro-links">
      <a class="button primary" href={home + "labs/wireshark/"}>
        Read the TCP lab
      </a>
      <a class="button" href={home + "#udp"}>
        Review UDP basics
      </a>
    </div>
    <ul class="lab-steps">
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.wireshark.org/docs/dfref/u/udp.html">
          Wireshark UDP display filter fields
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc768.html">
          RFC 768 · UDP header and length
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</CourseLayout>
